## 7. Frontend Architecture and Asset Management

### HTML

Each page should use:

- A valid HTML5 document structure
- A descriptive page title
- An appropriate meta description
- Semantic headings
- Accessible navigation
- Correct internal links
- Descriptive image alternative text where needed
- A consistent header and footer

### CSS — style.css

The shared stylesheet should manage global presentation where practical:

- Brand colors and typography
- Responsive page layouts
- Header and navigation
- Mobile navigation
- Buttons and calls to action
- Forms and validation messages
- Footer styling
- Accessibility and reduced-motion preferences

Keep page-specific CSS where necessary, but avoid unnecessary duplication or conflicting rules.

### JavaScript — script.js

The shared script may provide:

- Mobile navigation
- Active navigation states
- Smooth anchor scrolling
- Back-to-top behavior
- Automatic copyright year
- External-link handling
- Client-side form validation
- Image error handling
- Accessibility enhancements

Verify the actual implementation before relying on any feature. Load shared scripts only once per page and avoid duplicate event handlers.

### Asset JavaScript files

The current tree lists these files under `images/`:

- `images/images.js`
- `images/jobs.js`
- `images/applications.js`

Confirm each script's purpose and the HTML paths that load it. Do not move files without updating references.

### Image catalogue

If `images.js` manages image assets, keep its paths and references synchronized with actual files.

Do not assume every numbered image exists or uses the same extension. Verify filenames and extensions before updating references.

